[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
The site-wide outage is caused by a firewall/forwarding policy on router6 (the edge router for the 172.18.51.0/24 LAN). End-hosts on that LAN (e.g., pc15 172.18.51.15) can reach their local gateway 172.18.51.254, but cannot reach any external networks; conversely, remote sites cannot reach hosts on 172.18.51.0/24. Tests show pc15 -> 10.50.1.1 and pc15 -> 192.168.100.2 both time out, and pc11 -> 172.18.51.15 also times out. However, router6 itself can ping remote hosts (e.g., router6 -> 192.168.200.11 works), proving routing is up.

On router6, iptables is misconfigured: the FORWARD chain drops any packet entering via eth2 (the transit/WAN toward router3, 10.50.1.0/24): 
-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
-A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
-A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
This blocks all return traffic and any inbound sessions from the rest of the network, while outbound from LAN to WAN appears to “send” but never receives replies, matching user symptoms.

Localization: router6, interface eth2 (r6_r3), subsystem: Linux netfilter/iptables FORWARD chain. Root cause: an overly broad DROP rule on the FORWARD chain for all traffic arriving on eth2.

Corrective fix: remove/adjust these rules and allow established traffic from eth2:
- iptables -I FORWARD 1 -m state --state ESTABLISHED,RELATED -j ACCEPT
- Delete the blanket -i eth2 DROP rules or replace with specific, least-privilege ACLs.
As a quick restore: iptables -F FORWARD (if default policy is ACCEPT), then persist via netfilter-persistent/save. Afterward, verify pc15 can ping 10.50.1.1 and remote subnets. 
=== END ===